    "id": "i12",
    "chunks": [
      {
        "file": "/home/arnold/arnold/github/notes-helper/backend/projects/0728ff4c758a/refs/textbook.md",
        "start": 221,
        "end": 223,
        "text": "The most convenient way to understand linear operators is in terms of their matrix representations.\nIn fact, the linear operator and matrix viewpoints turn out to be completely equivalent.\nThe matrix viewpoint may be more familiar to you, however."
      },
      {
        "file": "/home/arnold/arnold/github/notes-helper/backend/projects/0728ff4c758a/refs/textbook.md",
        "start": 240,
        "end": 251,
        "text": "Suppose $A: V \\rightarrow W$ is a linear operator between vector spaces $V$ and $W$.\nSuppose $\\left|v_{1}\\right\\rangle, \\ldots,\\left|v_{m}\\right\\rangle$ is a basis for $V$ and $\\left|w_{1}\\right\\rangle, \\ldots,\\left|w_{n}\\right\\rangle$ is a basis for $W$.\nThen for each $j$ in the range $1, \\ldots, m$, there exist complex numbers $A_{1 j}$ through $A_{n j}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{equation*}\nA\\left|v_{j}\\right\\rangle=\\sum_{i} A_{i j}\\left|w_{i}\\right\\rangle \\tag{2.12}\n\\end{equation*}\n$$\n\nThe matrix whose entries are the values $A_{i j}$ is said to form a matrix representation of the operator $A$."
      }
