        "start": 928,
        "end": 931,
        "text": "The trace is easily seen to be cyclic, $\\operatorname{tr}(A B)=\\operatorname{tr}(B A)$, and linear, $\\operatorname{tr}(A+B)= \\operatorname{tr}(A)+\\operatorname{tr}(B), \\operatorname{tr}(z A)=z \\operatorname{tr}(A)$, where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary matrices, and $z$ is a complex number.\nFurthermore, from the cyclic property it follows that the trace of a matrix is invariant under the unitary similarity transformation $A \\rightarrow U A U^{\\dagger}$, as $\\operatorname{tr}\\left(U A U^{\\dagger}\\right)= \\operatorname{tr}\\left(U^{\\dagger} U A\\right)=\\operatorname{tr}(A)$.\nIn light of this result, it makes sense to define the trace of an operator $A$ to be the trace of any matrix representation of $A$."
